title: R1_D2_ArthurCaseA
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #char_Supervisor
---
Arthur tilts the dashboard closer: engagement curving up nicely, sanity jagged.
Arthur: Here's what they see: engagement up, sanity… flexible. Directors scroll, stop, go "Who's this one?"
Player: Is that good?
Arthur: Depends. For me? Means my boss might stop asking if I made a hiring mistake. For you? Means they're wondering how much more we can get away with.
He flicks to a technical panel.
Arthur: R&D's whispering about a new controller. Hungrier, looser bumpers. Learns fast from "high performers," doesn't freak when sanity dips.
Arthur studies you.
Arthur: Plug your lane into the pilot and Timmy's guardrails get thinner. Your moves hit harder. Your leaderboard dot climbs faster. Catch: you become the chew toy for every lab curious about kids without safety protocols.
He slides a choice UI between you.
Arthur: Fast, sharp version of the job or slightly dull one?
-> Opt into the aggressive controller.
    <<set $arthur_vector = "faustian">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice_set = "set1">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice = "faustian">>
    <<set $aggressive_controller = true>>
    <<set $trust_supervisor += 1>>
    <<set $engagement += 2>>
    <<set $sanity -= 2>>
    Player: If this is how the game is played… plug me in.
    Arthur watches, nods slowly, checks a box labeled "Pilot – High-Variance Controller," signs with a lazy finger-scribble.
    Arthur: I'll tell them you volunteered for "innovation." Makes everyone feel better.
    Player: How bad does it get?
    Arthur: Good day, you feel like a wizard. Bad day, you sneeze and his semester falls over. You're now a different color on three dashboards. Try not to make me explain that color to Compliance.
    Arthur softens a hair.
    Arthur: You clearly get the engagement side. Remember there's a kid under the graph. Fry him outright and no controller makes it look good.
    <<jump R1_D2_PlannerUnlock>>
-> Stick with the standard controller.
    <<set $arthur_vector = "hesitant_optimizer">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice_set = "set1">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice = "heros_journey">>
    <<set $aggressive_controller = false>>
    <<set $sanity += 1>>
    <<set $engagement -= 1>>
    Player: I think I'll hit my numbers without being a lab rat.
    Arthur smirks.
    Arthur: Wow. A cautious one. Standard controller it is. Life gets ten percent less exciting and thirty percent more survivable.
    Player: Mistake?
    Arthur: Depends if you want to be a leaderboard legend or someone who sleeps at night. You clearly have good instincts. You don't need spicy hardware to prove it.
    He taps a note: "shows prudent judgment."
    Arthur: Officially I'll say that. Unofficially: "might not burn the kid down for an A/B test." I like having one of those.
    <<jump R1_D2_PlannerUnlock>>

===

title: R1_D2_ArthurCaseB
tags: #run_1 #day_2 #scene #char_Supervisor
---
Arthur zooms in on the bars: sanity pretty, engagement not so much.
Arthur: Huh. You… let him sleep.
Player: He was wrecked. I didn't want him to fall apart on day one.
Arthur: I get it. I have a son. I've watched that exact kid stumble through first semester.
He taps Engagement.
Arthur: Corporate looks at this slope and sees "user not sufficiently activated." "Untapped potential." Their words. You can't keep a conscience and this job forever. Not in pure form.
Player: That's not a lot of options.
Arthur holds up two fingers.
Arthur: Option one: walk the tightrope. Stay clever. Keep him mostly okay and the graphs tolerable. Give me wins so I can say "long-term retention strategy" with a straight face.
He folds one finger.
Arthur: Option two: stop fighting the current. Run the company playbook, hit engagement targets, make peace with "sanity" in eight-point font.
Player: Those both sound bad.
Arthur: Welcome to tech. Refusing to choose just means you're replaced by someone who already picked option two.
-> Try to walk the tightrope.
    <<set $arthur_vector = "tightrope">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice_set = "set2">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice = "icarus">>
    <<set $aggressive_controller = false>>
    <<set $engagement += 1>>
    <<set $sanity += 1>>
    Player: If there's a strip where he's not wrecked and the graphs don't tank, I at least want to try.
    Arthur exhales a laugh.
    Arthur: You're going to give me gray hair.
    Player: You already have gray hair.
    Arthur: Exactly. Correlation, meet causation.
    He scribbles "nuanced tradeoff strategies" into the report.
    Arthur: When you do something that costs engagement, make it interesting. Give me a story about why it'll pay off later.
    <<jump R1_D2_PlannerUnlock>>
-> Commit to the company playbook.
    <<set $arthur_vector = "playbook">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice_set = "set2">>
    <<set $r1_arc_choice = "anti_hero">>
    <<set $aggressive_controller = false>>
    <<set $engagement += 2>>
    <<set $sanity -= 1>>
    Player: You're right. I don't get to be a hero and keep this job. I'll run the playbook.
    Arthur doesn't exactly smile, but his shoulders loosen.
    Arthur: Honest answer. Follow the scripts, hit the targets, don't make waves unless I ask.
    He scrolls "Standard Youth Segment Best Practices."
    Arthur: You see a "good for him, bad for us" option, you log it, maybe try it once, then move on. Bleak? Sure. But that's a mortgage payment and health insurance.
    Arthur shrugs.
    Arthur: I'm not going to yell at you for helping the kid now and then. I'm just setting expectations. Keep the line decent and sneak in mercies where no one can see them, maybe I can protect you.
    <<jump R1_D2_PlannerUnlock>>

===

